# Source for q/ae-views-measure5.js: Measurements (reproducibility, AF, respiratory %, indexing, reports) + Echo views (orientation, sequences) + Stress echo.
M="Measurements"; V="Echo views"; S="Stress echo"
Q=[
# --- Reproducibility and comparison ---
(M,"The test–retest variability of 2D biplane LVEF is roughly:","About 5 to 10 percentage points",["Under 1 point","About 30 points","Zero"],"Small changes in EF between studies may not be real.","C"),
(M,"Compared with 2D EF, GLS usually has:","Better reproducibility between readers",["Worse reproducibility","No reproducibility","The same values on all vendors"],"Use the same vendor for follow-up strain.","F"),
(M,"When comparing a current study with a previous one, the best practice is to:","Review the old and new images side by side, not just the reported numbers",["Read only the old report","Ignore the old study","Copy the old measurements"],"Side-by-side review reduces false changes.","F"),
(M,"Interobserver variability means:","Differences between measurements made by different people",["Differences in one person's repeat measurements","Machine errors only","Differences in patients"],"Intraobserver variability is one person repeating the measurement.","F"),
(M,"For serial studies in a patient getting chemotherapy, it is best to use:","The same method, machine vendor and, if possible, the same lab",["A different method every time","Any machine","Only visual EF"],"This reduces measurement differences unrelated to the patient.","F"),
# --- AF and respiratory variation ---
(M,"In atrial fibrillation, the 'index beat' method measures Doppler on:","The beat that follows two nearly equal R–R intervals",["The shortest beat","The first beat after a pause","Any random beat"],"It gives a single beat that represents average values.","C"),
(M,"Without the index beat method, Doppler in AF is usually averaged over:","At least 5 beats (often 5 to 10)",["1 beat","2 beats","50 beats"],"Beat-to-beat variation is large in AF.","F"),
(M,"Mitral E is 100 cm/s in expiration and 70 cm/s in inspiration. Respiratory variation is:","30%",["70%","100%","15%"],"(100 − 70) ÷ 100 = 30%. More than 25% suggests tamponade or constriction.","C"),
(M,"Tricuspid E rises from 50 cm/s in expiration to 75 cm/s in inspiration. The change is:","50% increase",["25% increase","75% increase","No change"],"(75 − 50) ÷ 50 = 50%. Over 40% supports tamponade.","C"),
(M,"Normal respiratory variation of mitral E in a healthy person is usually under about:","10% to 15%",["50%","40%","100%"],"Large variation suggests pericardial disease, lung disease or severe asthma.","C"),
# --- Indexing ---
(M,"Indexing to BSA can underestimate chamber enlargement in obese patients because:","BSA increases with weight more than the heart does",["The heart always grows with weight","BSA is not affected by weight","BSA uses age only"],"Some labs also index to height in obesity.","C"),
(M,"For the aorta, indexing to height (rather than BSA) is useful in:","Tall, very short or obese patients and some genetic aortic conditions",["All children only","Mitral stenosis","Patients with normal height only"],"The aortic height index is used in some guidelines.","C"),
(M,"In children, echo measurements are usually reported as:","Z-scores compared with normal children of the same body size",["Adult reference values","Raw numbers only","Percent of adult size"],"A Z-score over +2 means larger than normal.","F"),
(M,"A Z-score of +3 for a coronary artery in a child with Kawasaki disease means:","The artery is dilated (aneurysm range starts at about +2.5)",["Normal size","Smaller than normal","The test failed"],"Z-scores guide risk and follow-up.","C"),
(M,"Reference ranges for LV size and mass are:","Different for men and women",["The same for all adults","Only used in children","Based on height only"],"Use sex-specific ranges when reporting.","F"),
# --- TAVR and 3D ---
(M,"The preferred method for sizing the aortic annulus before TAVR is:","CT angiography (3D TEE is an alternative)",["2D TTE only","Chest X-ray","ECG"],"The annulus is oval, so 2D can underestimate its size.","F"),
(M,"3D vena contracta area of a regurgitant jet is useful because:","Many regurgitant orifices are not round",["All orifices are round","It removes the need for color","It measures velocity"],"2D vena contracta measures only one dimension.","C"),
(M,"Mitral valve area by 3D planimetry is useful because it lets the sonographer:","Choose the true smallest orifice plane at the leaflet tips",["Avoid using color","Measure only in systole","Measure the aorta"],"2D cuts may be off-axis.","F"),
(M,"Before percutaneous mitral or tricuspid procedures, 3D TEE helps by showing:","Valve anatomy en face, like a surgeon's view",["Only coronary arteries","Only the LV apex","Lung tissue"],"It helps choose patients and plan devices.","F"),
# --- Comprehensive reporting ---
(M,"A complete aortic stenosis report should include:","Peak velocity, mean gradient, valve area, DVI, LVOT diameter, EF and stroke volume index",["Only the peak velocity","Only LV mass","Only the heart rate"],"Integrating measures avoids mistakes in grading.","F"),
(M,"A complete MR assessment should combine:","Leaflet anatomy, jet features, vena contracta, PISA, pulmonary vein flow and LV/LA size",["Only jet area","Only heart rate","Only blood pressure"],"No single measure is enough.","F"),
(M,"RV systolic function is best described using:","Several measures, such as TAPSE, s′, FAC and RV strain",["Only the RV diameter","Only the IVC","Only LV EF"],"Each measure has different strengths.","F"),
(M,"LA function can be measured with:","LA strain, LA emptying fraction and pulmonary vein and mitral A-wave Doppler",["Only LA diameter","Only LV mass","Only aortic root size"],"Reduced LA function is linked to AF and HFpEF.","C"),
(M,"LA emptying fraction is calculated as:","(Maximum LA volume − minimum LA volume) ÷ maximum LA volume",["LA volume ÷ BSA","LA diameter ÷ LV diameter","E ÷ e′"],"It reflects overall LA function.","C"),
(M,"Reporting blood pressure with an echo is important because:","Many measurements, such as LV mass, gradients and MR severity, change with blood pressure",["It is not useful","It replaces the ECG","It is only for billing"],"High BP can make MR look worse and AS gradients look lower.","F"),
(M,"Heart rate should be reported with an MS gradient because:","Faster heart rates shorten diastole and raise the gradient",["Heart rate has no effect","It lowers the gradient","It measures valve area"],"Compare gradients at similar heart rates.","F"),
# --- More measurement details ---
(M,"The LVOT obstruction gradient in HCM is measured with CW from the:","Apical five-chamber or three-chamber view",["Suprasternal view","Subcostal IVC view","PLAX only"],"Avoid contamination from the MR jet.","F"),
(M,"LV internal dimensions by 2D are measured:","At the level of the mitral leaflet tips (or just below), perpendicular to the LV long axis",["At the apex","At the aortic valve","At the papillary muscles in PSAX only"],"2D linear measurements are preferred over M-mode when the M-mode cursor cannot be placed correctly.","F"),
(M,"Wall thickness should exclude:","RV trabeculations, the moderator band and false tendons",["The septum","The LV cavity","The pericardium only"],"Including these structures overestimates thickness.","F"),
(M,"The coronary sinus diameter is normally under about:","1 cm",["3 cm","5 cm","0.1 cm"],"A dilated coronary sinus suggests a left SVC or high RA pressure.","C"),
(M,"Mitral annular diameter in diastole over about 35 mm or annular dilation can contribute to:","Functional MR",["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Coarctation"],"Annular rings are used in repair.","C"),
(M,"Aortic valve calcification on echo is graded by:","How much of the cusps are bright and whether cusp motion is reduced",["The LV size","The heart rate","The IVC size"],"CT calcium scoring is more objective.","F"),
(M,"Stroke volume calculated at the LVOT and at the RVOT should normally be:","About equal, unless there is a shunt or regurgitation",["Very different","Always zero","Twice as large on the right"],"This forms the basis of Qp/Qs.","F"),
(M,"For pulmonary vein Doppler, the Ar duration is compared with the:","Mitral A-wave duration",["Mitral E velocity","LVOT VTI","TR velocity"],"Ar longer than A by more than 30 ms suggests raised LVEDP.","F"),
(M,"Hepatic vein Doppler is recorded with the patient:","Breathing quietly, ideally with a respiratory trace",["Doing a Valsalva only","Running","Holding breath for one minute"],"Changes with breathing are key in constriction.","F"),
(M,"Measuring the TR jet from more than one window is recommended because:","The highest, best-aligned velocity gives the most accurate RVSP",["It reduces aliasing","It changes RA pressure","It shows the PA"],"Report the highest clear velocity.","F"),
(M,"For aortic root measurements, an oblique cut through the aorta in PLAX will:","Overestimate the diameter",["Underestimate the diameter","Have no effect","Measure the LVOT"],"Find the largest diameter perpendicular to the long axis.","F"),
(M,"When an EF is reported visually, the report should:","State that it is a visual estimate, often as a range (for example 55% to 60%)",["Give a single exact number","Not mention it","Use LV mass instead"],"Visual EF is acceptable for experienced readers with good images.","F"),
(M,"In a technically difficult study, measurements that cannot be made reliably should be:","Reported as not measurable or limited, not guessed",["Estimated without comment","Copied from an old report","Left blank without explanation"],"Clear limits prevent mistakes in care.","F"),
# --- Views: orientation and sequences ---
(V,"For the PSAX view, the transducer marker points toward the patient's:","Left shoulder",["Right shoulder","Feet","Back"],"From PLAX, rotate about 90° clockwise.","F"),
(V,"For the standard apical four-chamber view, the transducer marker usually points toward the patient's:","Left side (about 3 o'clock)",["Right shoulder","Head","Feet"],"This places the LV on the right side of the screen.","F"),
(V,"From the apical four-chamber view, the apical two-chamber view is obtained by rotating the transducer:","Counterclockwise about 60°",["Clockwise 90°","180°","Not rotating, only tilting"],"Continue rotating for the apical long-axis view.","F"),
(V,"For the subcostal four-chamber view, the transducer marker usually points toward the patient's:","Left side",["Head","Feet","Right shoulder"],"The probe is held almost flat on the abdomen.","F"),
(V,"From subcostal four-chamber, rotating about 90° counterclockwise gives the:","Subcostal short-axis views",["Apical two-chamber view","PLAX","Suprasternal view"],"Sweeping shows the great vessels and ventricles.","C"),
(V,"An apical view where the LV is on the left side of the screen is:","A reversed (Mayo) orientation used by some labs",["Always an error","A subcostal view","A PSAX view"],"Each lab should use one orientation consistently.","C"),
(V,"A good habit at the start of each view is to:","Optimize depth, gain and focus before recording",["Record first and adjust later","Use maximum depth always","Use the same gain for every patient"],"Optimized images make measurements accurate.","F"),
(V,"In a sweep from base to apex in PSAX, the sonographer moves:","From the aortic valve level, to the mitral valve, papillary muscles, then apex",["From the apex to the aorta only","Only at the mitral level","Across the subcostal window"],"Tilt or slide toward the apex.","F"),
(V,"A 'zoomed' view of the aortic valve and LVOT is recorded mainly to:","Measure the LVOT diameter accurately",["Measure LV mass","Look at the IVC","Measure the RV"],"Zoom improves the precision of small measurements.","F"),
(V,"In a patient with dextrocardia, apical views are found:","On the right side of the chest",["On the left side as normal","In the abdomen","On the back"],"Transducer orientation is also reversed.","F"),
(V,"For a patient after recent chest surgery with dressings, the most useful window is often:","Subcostal or apical, avoiding the incision",["Directly over the incision","Suprasternal only","None"],"Use sterile technique near wounds.","F"),
(V,"When imaging the aortic arch from the suprasternal window, the sonographer should avoid:","Pressing too hard on the neck",["Using any gel","Tilting the probe","Recording Doppler"],"The area is sensitive and pressure can be uncomfortable.","F"),
(V,"An echo protocol 'limited study' means:","A focused exam that answers a specific question, not a full study",["A study with poor images only","A pediatric study","A TEE only"],"The report should say it was limited.","F"),
(V,"Point-of-care cardiac ultrasound (POCUS) is mainly used to:","Answer quick, focused questions at the bedside, such as effusion or LV function",["Replace full echo in all cases","Measure valve areas","Perform strain"],"Abnormal findings may need a full echo.","F"),
(V,"In the PLAX view, the best way to show the RVOT, aortic valve and LA in a line is to:","Keep the aortic root and LA aligned horizontally with the LV",["Tilt toward the apex","Rotate 90°","Move to the subcostal window"],"Fine tilting brings the aortic valve into the center of the image.","F"),
# --- Stress echo ---
(S,"Before stress echo, patients usually should not:","Eat a heavy meal or take beta-blockers (unless told to continue them)",["Drink any water","Bring their medication list","Wear comfortable shoes"],"Beta-blockers can blunt heart rate response.","F"),
(S,"Peak images during treadmill stress echo are compared mainly with:","Rest images in the same views",["Images from a different patient","CT images","Only recovery images"],"Side-by-side review shows new abnormalities.","F"),
(S,"A stress echo for valve disease often adds:","Doppler at each stage, such as gradients, TR velocity and MR",["Only wall motion","Only ECG","Nothing extra"],"Valve stress tests answer specific questions about severity and symptoms.","F"),
(S,"In stress echo, an 'ischemic response' in a segment means it becomes:","Hypokinetic, akinetic or dyskinetic compared with rest",["More hyperkinetic","Unchanged and normal","Thicker in diastole"],"Normal segments become hyperkinetic.","F"),
(S,"Patients should be told before a treadmill stress test to:","Wear comfortable shoes and clothes",["Wear high heels","Avoid all fluids for a day","Bring no medication list"],"Good preparation improves safety and results.","F"),
(S,"A stress test that stops early for leg fatigue at 70% of target heart rate is called:","Submaximal (non-diagnostic for ischemia if negative)",["Maximal","Positive","Normal"],"Consider dobutamine stress if exercise is limited.","F"),
(S,"During dobutamine stress, a new regional abnormality at low dose that resolves at higher dose is most consistent with:","An artifact or poor image quality, and should be reviewed carefully",["Severe ischemia","Scar","Normal biphasic response"],"Ischemia usually worsens as stress increases.","C"),
(S,"An exercise duration of under 6 minutes on the Bruce protocol suggests:","Reduced exercise capacity",["Excellent fitness","Normal fitness for an athlete","No meaning"],"Exercise capacity is a strong predictor of outcome.","C"),
(S,"Leg cramps, unsteadiness or severe shortness of breath during a treadmill test should lead the sonographer to:","Help stop the test safely and alert the supervising physician",["Speed up the treadmill","Ignore them","Take more images first"],"Patient safety comes first.","F"),
(S,"A stress echo report should include:","Protocol, peak heart rate and % target, BP response, symptoms, ECG changes and wall motion findings",["Only the final diagnosis","Only images","Only heart rate"],"Complete reports help clinicians interpret results.","F"),
]
